  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 AM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nA cold front passed through the region last night and brought a \nreinforcing shot of cold air advection. Overcast clouds are moving \nsouth at this hour and are expected to blanket the region this \nmorning. Deep troughing will remain over the region today and \ncontinuous vorticity lobes will move in from the north in the \nmorning, which will promote lift and bring additional moisture. \nOngoing drizzle and light rain over southern Ohio will continue to \nwork south and approach the Bluegrass region in the early morning \nhours. By late morning, widely scattered rain showers will move in \nfrom central Indiana. The main precipitation type will be rain, \nhowever, if we see a heavier downpour, we could see rain/snow mix or \ngraupel. This is due to lower freezing levels (around 2000-3000ft), \nsteep lapse rates, and cooler temperatures within showers. \nOtherwise, the tight pressure gradient will keep breezy NW winds \naround 10-15mph today. \n\nShowers are expected to push southeast in the evening hours, as the \nupper troughing and energy begin to push east. We will see clearing \nskies from NW to SE overnight. The pressure gradient will begin to \nweaken, therefore, winds will become light overnight. Given these \nconditions, low temperatures on Saturday morning will be in the mid-\nupper 20s across most of the area, with urban areas in the low 30s. \n\nSaturday - Mid Next Week...\n\nDeep troughing will continue to push off to the northeast on \nSaturday. Strong surface high pressure will build in from the \nnorthwest and strengthen through the weekend. This will allow for \ndrier and clearer conditions. Though, continued fetch off of Lake \nMichigan may keep some clouds over southern Indiana and northern \nKentucky through part of Saturday. \n\nBuilding ridging will allow heights to increase each day through mid \nnext week. Therefore, high temperatures will increase 4-5 degrees \neach day, reaching the 60s by mid week. Dry conditions, calm winds, \nand clear skies will allow overnight temperatures to remain in the \n20s and 30s through most of this long term period. Mid next week, \nample mixing and warmer afternoon temperatures may bring minimum \nafternoon RH into the 20-30% range.",
